### Is there an existing issue for this?

- [X] I have searched the existing issues

### Describe the bug

In a statically rendered form (`[SupplyParameterFromForm]`, `EditForm` with `FormName`), a model property of type `S?`, where `S` is a custom struct implementing `IParsable<S>`, cannot be left empty: the browser posts `Model.Code=`, and the form mapping reports `The value '' is not valid for 'Code'.`, so the form is invalid. An `int?` property posted empty in the same form maps to `null` without error.

The cause is `NullableConverter<T>.TryConvertValue` ([main](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/4aa5ff64f3f6b3508573d4282fcd2b01cff03500/src/Components/Endpoints/src/FormMapping/Converters/NullableConverter.cs#L15-L38)). Since #60498, an empty value becomes `null` only when `IsSupportedUnderlyingType(typeof(T))` holds, that is when `T` has a `TypeCode` other than `Object` or is in the allowlist of [`IsSupportedUnderlyingObjectType`](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/4aa5ff64f3f6b3508573d4282fcd2b01cff03500/src/Components/Endpoints/src/FormMapping/Converters/NullableConverter.cs#L59-L74) (`DateOnly`, `TimeOnly`, `DateTimeOffset`, and since #66625 `TimeSpan`, `Guid`, `Int128`, `UInt128`, `Half`). Any other struct gets the empty text passed to `ParsableConverter<T>`, which calls `T.TryParse("", ...)` and [records a mapping error](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/4aa5ff64f3f6b3508573d4282fcd2b01cff03500/src/Components/Endpoints/src/FormMapping/Converters/ParsableConverter.cs#L13-L26) when it returns `false`.

#60498 describes this as deliberate: a custom `IParsable<T>` type "can provide custom implementation for handling empty/null inputs". But `TryParse` produces a `T`, not a `T?`, so the best it can do is succeed with some `T` value: the property then has a value, never `null`. The repro's `Tag`, whose `TryParse` accepts empty text, maps to `Tag("")`. There is no way for a custom struct to make an empty field mean "no value" for `S?`, which is what the empty input means for every allowlisted type, and what `InputBase<TValue>` does for any `Nullable<T>` in interactive rendering ([`InputBase.CurrentValueAsString`](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/4aa5ff64f3f6b3508573d4282fcd2b01cff03500/src/Components/Web/src/Forms/InputBase.cs#L131) sets `null` for empty text before any parsing).

This affects value objects and strongly typed identifiers (`readonly struct OrderId : IParsable<OrderId>`) used as optional fields of a static SSR form. The converters are internal, so the application cannot supply a different behaviour (#66731), and the only workaround is a `string` property parsed by hand. The same converter serves minimal APIs' `[FromForm]` complex types: on 10.0, posting `Code=` to `([FromForm] M m)` with `Code? Code` returns 400 while `Number=` binds `null`.

On 10.0, `Guid?`, `TimeSpan?`, `Int128?`, `UInt128?` and `Half?` are refused the same way (#66607): #66625 fixed them on `main` and the fix is in 11.0.0-rc.1, but `release/10.0` still has the [three-type allowlist](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/b6da38d94a8e132a8be53ee90afa8c04483f84c1/src/Components/Endpoints/src/FormMapping/Converters/NullableConverter.cs#L59-L67). The repro shows both.

### Expected Behavior

An empty value posted for an `S?` member, where `S` is any struct the form mapping can convert, maps to `null` without a mapping error, as it does for `int?`, `DateOnly?` or (on 11.0) `Guid?`.

If the behaviour #60498 introduced must be kept for a type whose `TryParse("")` succeeds, a compatible change would be to fall back to `null` only when the underlying converter refuses the empty value, instead of reporting the error. Something along these lines in `NullableConverter<T>.TryConvertValue`, as a sketch:

```csharp
if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(value))
{
    if (IsSupportedUnderlyingType(typeof(T)))
    {
        result = null;
        return true;
    }

    // A custom type keeps the first word on empty text (#60498); if it refuses it, the field was left empty, not invalid.
    var errorHandler = reader.ErrorHandler;
    reader.ErrorHandler = static (_, _, _) => { };
    try
    {
        result = ((ISingleValueConverter<T>)_nonNullableConverter).TryConvertValue(ref reader, value, out var converted) ? converted : null;
        return true;
    }
    finally
    {
        reader.ErrorHandler = errorHandler;
    }
}
```

As far as I can tell, this would change nothing for the types that map today, nor for the tests #60498 added (`ParsableTestStruct` still maps to a value), and turns only the current mapping error into `null`.

### Steps To Reproduce

Repro: https://github.com/AdCodicem/dotnet-upstream-repros/tree/main/aspnetcore-blazor-nullable-parsable

A Blazor Web App with no interactivity and no package beyond the shared framework. The page:

```razor
@page "/"

<EditForm Model="Model" FormName="repro" OnSubmit="Submit">
    <input name="Model.Code" value="@Model!.Code" />
    <input name="Model.Number" value="@Model!.Number" />
    <input name="Model.Id" value="@Model!.Id" />
    <input name="Model.Tag" value="@Model!.Tag?.Value" />
</EditForm>

@code {
    [SupplyParameterFromForm]
    public ReproModel? Model { get; set; }

    protected override void OnInitialized() => Model ??= new();

    private void Submit(EditContext context)
    {
        var valid = context.Validate(); // copies the form mapping errors into the EditContext
        // prints Model's members, valid and context.GetValidationMessages()
    }

    public sealed class ReproModel
    {
        public Code? Code { get; set; }   // Code : IParsable<Code>, three upper-case letters
        public int? Number { get; set; }
        public Guid? Id { get; set; }
        public Tag? Tag { get; set; }     // Tag : IParsable<Tag>, whose TryParse accepts empty text
    }
}
```

`Program.cs` starts the app, gets the antiforgery token and cookie from `GET /`, and posts the form as a browser does with every input left empty (`_handler=repro&Model.Code=&Model.Number=&Model.Id=&Model.Tag=`), then with every input filled. `./repro.sh` runs it on .NET 10, `REPRO_TFM=net11.0 ./repro.sh` on .NET 11.

Output on 10.0.13:

```
POST / (every input left empty) -> 200
Code   (Code?) = null
Number (int?)  = null
Id     (Guid?) = null
Tag    (Tag?)  = Tag("")
valid: False
mapping errors: The value '' is not valid for 'Code'. | The value '' is not valid for 'Id'.

POST / (every input filled) -> 200
Code   (Code?) = ABC
Number (int?)  = 7
Id     (Guid?) = 6f9619ff-8b86-d011-b42d-00c04fc964ff
Tag    (Tag?)  = Tag("x")
valid: True
mapping errors: none
```

Output on 11.0.0-rc.1.26425.128 (`Guid?` fixed by #66625, `Code?` still refused):

```
POST / (every input left empty) -> 200
Code   (Code?) = null
Number (int?)  = null
Id     (Guid?) = null
Tag    (Tag?)  = Tag("")
valid: False
mapping errors: The value '' is not valid for 'Code'.
```

### Exceptions (if any)

None. The refusal is a form mapping error: `The value '' is not valid for 'Code'.` (and `... for 'Id'.` on 10.0).

### .NET Version

10.0.402 (runtime 10.0.13); also 11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128

### Anything else?

- ASP.NET Core: 10.0.13 (`Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Endpoints` 10.0.13+4099f83977f4d29604a9aeb971fb0a7bc1d5f699) and 11.0.0-rc.1.26425.128
- No IDE involved: built and run with the .NET CLI on Windows 11 (10.0.26200), x64.
- Related: #52195 and #52499 (empty value to `null` for nullable value types), #60498 (the allowlist), #66607 and #66625 (BCL types missing from the allowlist), #55202 and #65754 (the same question for minimal APIs), #66731 (making the form converters public).
- Could #66625 be backported to 10.0? It is a regression introduced by #60498 that 10.0 still ships.

Investigation and write-up assisted by Claude (Anthropic); the linked repro was run on Windows 11 x64 with .NET SDK 10.0.402 and 11.0.100-rc.1.26425.128, and I reviewed this report.

